+## Value formats
+
+Format ids work anywhere VaporView takes a `numberFormat`/`valueFormat` id, such as `setDisplayFormat` or saved state:
+
+| id | Display |
+|---|---|
+| `riscv` | objdump default output: ABI names and pseudo-instructions (`li a0,10`, `ret`, `beqz a0,pc-16`, `csrr t6,mcycle`) |
+| `riscv_numeric` | the same with `x0..x31` names (objdump `-M numeric`) |
+| `riscv_noalias` | no pseudo-instructions (objdump `-M no-aliases`, so `addi a0,zero,10` and `c.li` forms) |
+| `riscv_numeric_noalias` | both options |
+| `alias:<table>` | value replaced by the name from alias table `<table>` (see below) |
+
+- Signals of 16 bits or wider are accepted. Only the low 32 bits are decoded. When bits [1:0] are not `11`, the low 16 bits are decoded as a compressed instruction. On a 16-bit signal, an encoding that is not compressed shows as hex.
+- Encodings the disassembler does not recognize show as hex (`0xffffffff`). Values that contain x/z show as `x`.
+- The waveform has no PC, so branch and jump targets display relative to the instruction: `pc+12`, `pc-4`. When a PC is passed in (`opts.pc`), they print as absolute addresses, the way objdump prints them. The tests use this mode.
+- Deliberate difference from objdump: RV32C shift encodings with shamt[5]=1 are reserved on RV32, so they show as hex. objdump decodes them anyway.
+- objdump's style is followed for edge cases: an empty fence set prints as `unknown`, HINTs print as `c.nop 5`, `c.li zero,..`, `c.slli zero,..`, `c.mv zero,..`, `c.add zero,..`, `c.slli64`, and `c.addi16sp sp,0` prints as `addi sp,sp,0`. Fences with nonzero rd/rs1 or a reserved fm show as hex, because objdump 2.43.1 also calls them unknown (the spec says to execute them as a normal fence).
+- The value search box matches the disassembled text as a case-insensitive regex. The regex is compiled once per search; text that is not a valid regex is matched as a plain substring.
+- CSR names: every name objdump 2.43.1 prints by default (426 CSRs, including vector, Smstateen, Sstc, AIA, hypervisor and debug). The table was produced by disassembling `csrrs` for all 4096 CSR numbers, and the test corpus checks all 4096.
+
+## Value aliases
+
+Supported alias file formats:
+
+- **Verdi nWave alias file** (any extension except .json/.csv/.rc). The syntax is nWave's alias-table format (an `alias ... endalias` table per name, plus the alias lines a Verdi resource file may hold), as described below.
+  - Supported: `alias NAME ... endalias` blocks (keywords are case-insensitive), the older block style where a line holding only a name starts a table, and entries outside any block (they go into a table named after the file). Entries are `name value [colorID]`. A keyword counts only when it is unquoted, has the right number of arguments and (for block starts) sits outside an explicit block. Inside an `alias` block, a line holding only a name is a warning ("has no value"), not a new table. A missing `endalias` at the end of the file is also a warning. A value is a Verilog literal (`8'h1f`, `'b1?0?`, `4'd9`), a range `lo~hi` (spaces allowed, may be quoted), or `others`. Names with spaces are double-quoted.
+  - Comments: whole-line `#` and `//`. As an extension, an unquoted `#...` or `//...` after the first token is a trailing comment (Verdi documents only comment lines). The exception is a `#rgb`/`#rrggbb` token that ends the line, which is a color. Colors are `ID_*`, `NULL` or `#rgb`.
+  - Also supported: `slice NAME ... endslice`, where each row is `msb[:lsb] [table|radix]`. Rows display left to right, separated by spaces. A reversed range is shown bit-reversed. A field containing x/z shows `*`. Also: and `curAliasTable`.
+  - Resource-file alias lines may also appear in an alias file, mixed with blocks: `aliasmapname/nalias`, `slicemapname/nslice`, and `aliasname X` + the next `addSignal ... /path/sig[..]`. The last pair is a binding to `path.sig`; `aliasname` applies to the next signal only. An `aliasmapname`/`slicemapname` block ends at the first line that is not one of its `nalias`/`nslice` rows.
+  - **Verdi resource files** (`.rc`): only the alias lines above are read. All other commands are ignored without warnings.
+  - Verdi compares a value that has no base in the signal's current radix (hex by default). Bare numbers here therefore default to hex. Pass `bareRadix: 'dec'|'bin'|'oct'` to override.
+  - `condAlias` (conditional on other signals) is skipped with a warning. Colors are parsed and kept but not drawn. VaporView draws a bus in one color per signal.
+- **JSON**: `{"tables": {"t": {"0": "IDLE", "0x2": "RUN", "3~5": "BUSY", "4'b1??1": "X", "others": "?"}}, "signals": {"top.cpu.state": "t", "top.cpu.instr": "riscv"}}`. A table can also be a list: `[{"value", "name", "color"}]`. An object without `tables` is read as the tables map. `slices` take the form `{"name": [{"range": "7:4", "table": "t"}]}`. Bare numbers are decimal. A `signals` value can be a table name or a built-in format id such as `riscv`.
+- **CSV**: rows of `value,name` or `value,name,#color` (one table named after the file; a 3-column row counts as value,name,color only when the third field is a `#rgb` color and the first parses as a value) or `table,value,name[,color]`, or a header row naming `table`, `signal`, `value`, `name`/`alias`, `color`. Warnings carry the line number in the file. When a `signal` column is present, each signal gets its own table and a binding. Bare numbers are decimal.
+- Matching works like this: the first matching entry (in file order) wins, and `others` is the fallback. `others` does not match x/z values. Unmatched values show as hex, with x/z nibbles shown as `x`/`z` like VaporView's hex format. A slice field holding x/z shows `*`, as in Verdi.
+- Each table is compiled once into an exact-value map plus an ordered list of wildcard and range entries. That takes 200k values against a 256-entry table from 268 ms to 27 ms.
+- Limits for untrusted files: a literal is at most 4096 bits (size or digits); larger ones are skipped with a warning. Parsing never throws: bad JSON shapes, invalid JSON and an unknown `bareRadix` all come back as warnings.
+- Alias names are returned as plain text (`rawText`). The canvas, search and clipboard get `R&W`, and the HTML value column escapes it there.
+- Loading a file again replaces tables with the same names and redraws the rows that use them, because the format reads the registry on every call.
+- Bindings (signal → format):
+  - When a file loads, they apply to every displayed signal named in that file, as one batch and one undo step (one `contextUpdate` with `StateChangeType.User`).
+  - Rows added later by `add-variable` get their binding right after VaporView creates them. The context update is sent with `StateChangeType.None`, so the binding belongs to the add's undo step.
+  - `apply-state` (session restore, Undo, Redo) never rebinds. The restored formats win, and the redo stack is kept.
+  - A format the user sets afterwards is never overridden.
+  - Rows are tracked by object (a `WeakSet`), not by rowId, because rowIds restart at 0 when the viewer empties.
+  - Formats are set directly (unset/set the cache, redraw), so a file that loads while the search box has focus still applies.
+
+## Host interface (for cmux's context menu)
+
+VaporView's VS Code commands (`vaporview.displayAsHexadecimal`, etc. in `src/extension_core/commands.ts`) all end up as one webview message. `viewer_provider.ts` `setValueFormat()` sends:
+
+```js
+{command: 'setDisplayFormat', netlistId, numberFormat: 'riscv'}   // or rowId instead of netlistId
+```
+
+This is handled by `rowHandler.setDisplayFormat` (webview `vscode_wrapper.ts`). If the target row is part of the current selection, the format applies to every selected row, the same as the VS Code menu. A host posts it the same way it posts `add-variable`. The right-clicked row's `netlistId`/`rowId` comes from the `data-vscode-context` of the clicked element, which is what VS Code's menu reads. The step-1 `window.cmuxWaveform.viewerContext` also exposes it.
+
+New messages, handled by `handleExtraMessage()` (`extra_formats.ts`), which `VscodeWrapper.handleMessage()` calls before its own switch. It consumes these three, so they are no longer logged as unknown:
+
+```js
+{command: 'loadValueAliases', fileName: 'cpu.alias', text: '<file contents>', bareRadix?: 'hex', apply?: true}
+{command: 'applyAliasBindings'}
+{command: 'clearValueAliases'}
+```
+
+The reply comes back through the existing webview→host channel as `{command: 'executeCommand', commandName: 'vaporview.valueAliasesLoaded', args: [{fileName, tables, formatIds, bindings, defaultTable, warnings, applied}]}`, or `{..., error}`. The webview cannot read files, so the host reads the file (local, or over SSH) and sends the text.
+
+A synchronous alternative for Swift `evaluateJavaScript`: `window.vaporviewValueFormats.loadValueAliases(text, fileName, {bareRadix, apply})`, `.clearValueAliases()`, `.applyAliasBindings()`, `.listFormats()`. `listFormats()` returns `[{id, label}]` for the four RISC-V formats plus `alias:<table>` for every loaded table, which is the list to build the menu from.
+
+Suggested menu: Value Format → RISC-V ▸ (ABI names / x-registers / no pseudo-instructions) → `setDisplayFormat`. Load Alias File… → host file picker → `loadValueAliases`. Alias ▸ <tables from listFormats()> → `setDisplayFormat` with `alias:<table>`. Remove Alias → `setDisplayFormat` with `hexadecimal`.
+
+Not done: VS Code `package.json` commands and menus for these formats (only the webview side and the host messages are implemented).
+
+The value-link event (`ValueLinkEvent.formattedValue`, sent to the host and to API consumers) carries plain text for the RISC-V and alias formats. Upstream formats that escape inside `formatString` (enum) send escaped text there; raw text is the consistent target if upstream changes later.
+
+Messages are not checked by origin or source, the same as upstream `vscode_wrapper.ts`. In VS Code and in cmux's WKWebView no other frame can post to the webview. If cmux ever embeds third-party iframes, check `e.source` in `handleMessage`.
