+## Exact value search
+
+Upstream's numeric `checkSearchValue` (`regexMatchBinary` in `value_format.ts`) tests the
+parsed search as an unanchored regex over the value's bits, so a hex search for 5
+("0101") also matched 0x15, 0x50 and 0x55. Verdi searches for the value itself.
+
+| File | Change |
+|---|---|
+| `src/webview/value_search.ts` | **New.** `matchBinaryExact`: upstream's regex, anchored to the whole value, with zero extension (sign extension for signed). A search longer than the signal matches when its extra high bits are that extension. Wildcards (`.`, hex/decimal `x`/`z`) and regexes still work, so `.*0101.*` is still a substring search. `signedSearchBits`: the signed format's parsed search keeps its sign bit, so +5 ("0101") is no longer -3 ("101"). Negative values are parsed as upstream. |
+| `src/webview/value_format.ts` | Hex, octal, binary, decimal, signed and the float formats use it; ASCII, string and enum searches are unchanged. |
+
+The windowed path (`toHostPattern` in `window_cache.ts`) already searched exact values; its
+text-format fallback calls `checkSearchValue`, so it is exact now too.
+`test/riscv/search.test.ts` (45 checks, run by `node test/riscv/run.cjs`) covers it.
+
+## Windowed-viewer review follow-ups (hybrid #5, #8; L1–L3)
+
+All in `src/webview/window_cache.ts`. The spike harness's `?ftest=1` (`followup-test.js`) checks each one.
+It was red before these changes.
+
+- **#5:** a bit slice added after its windowed source. The slice reads the source whole through the
+  v1 path, so a pending or running hybrid load of that source is dropped (`loadedWholeElsewhere`).
+  The source no longer crosses the wire twice.
+- **#8:** a navigation timeout's notice gives how long the user waited, not `longQueryMs`. A step
+  that joined a prefetch times out sooner.
+- **L1:** a user step that won't join an old prefetch now cancels it. Before, the step's new
+  `navAsk` entry left the old prefetch running until its own timeout.
+- **L2:** with no marker, a forward text-format search also tests record 0 (the value at time 0),
+  as upstream's search from index 0 does.
+- **L3:** if a signal is over the hybrid budget only because of loads still pending, it is decided
+  again later, not marked windowed for good.
+- **N1** (from the follow-ups' verify): a signal is also checked where a count or a hybrid load starts,
+  not only where it enters the cache (`settleIfV1`, `v1Whole`). If the v1 path holds the signal, or is
+  fetching it for a slice, it gets no count and no load. This covers a slice row restored before its
+  source row.
+
+## Text searches on windowed rows stay text
+
+`WindowCache.searchValue` (`src/webview/window_cache.ts`) used to try `toHostPattern` on every
+format's parsed search first. A search in a text format made only of `0`, `1`, `x`, `z`, `?`
+or `.` characters (RISC-V `x1` or `0`, an alias or enum name, a fixed-point or epoch number)
+became a bit pattern for the helper and found nothing, where a whole-signal row matches the
+text. Now only formats whose search is a bit pattern over the whole value (`matchExact` and
+`matchExactSigned`: binary, hex, octal, decimal, signed, floats; `searchesBitPattern` in
+`value_format.ts`) go to the helper as a pattern. Every other format (RISC-V, aliases, ASCII,
+string, enum, fixed point, epoch time) takes the formatted path (`searchFormatted`), which
+calls the format's own `checkSearchValue` as non-windowed rows do. cmux's `run.sh windowed`
+checks `x1` and `0` in RISC-V on a windowed row; it was red before this change.
+
+## Text searches on windowed rows run next to the dump (helper searchText)
+
+A value search in RISC-V, ASCII or an alias table on a windowed row used to fetch exact
+chunks of the signal and scan them in the viewer (`scanChunks`): on a long dump that is every
+change crossing the connection. Now `searchFormatted` (`window_cache.ts`) first asks the FSDB
+helper's `searchText` (WINDOWING.md), which formats each distinct value once with the
+viewer's own formatters and returns only the hit's time:
+- RISC-V (all four ids) and `alias:<table>` send the viewer's pattern (regex, else plain
+  text, case-insensitive). An alias table goes with the file it was read from
+  (`aliasSearchSource` in `extra_formats.ts`), and only when that one file holds the table
+  and every table its slices name; otherwise the page scan runs.
+- ASCII sends the text as typed (`ControlBar.rawSearchValue`); the helper turns it into the
+  same bit regex as `formatAscii.parseSearchValue`.
+- No marker: `t: 0, inclusive: true`, so a match at t=0 counts. Next/previous from the
+  marker are exclusive, as before.
+- A request that hits its budget (5 s) answers `incomplete` with a resume point; the search
+  resumes there (inclusive) until it ends or `longQueryMs` passes (then the timeout note).
+  Escape cancels the request in flight, as for other host searches.
+- The page scan still answers when the helper has no `searchText` (an unknown-command or
+  not-relayed error; not asked again until the file reloads), when it reports `tooLarge`, on
+  any other error, and for the other text formats (string, enum, fixed point, epoch).
+cmux's `run.sh windowed` checks t=0, far and no-match, resume, cancel, ASCII, alias, tooLarge
+and the unknown-command fallback against a mock that runs the shipped helper's `text.js`.
+
+## Split a bus into slice rows
+
+A variable row (netlist or custom) can own slices: custom bit-range variables kept in a
+`SliceGroup` (`src/webview/signal_item.ts`), a `SignalGroup` with no row of its own whose
+`owner` is the variable's row. The variable keeps its own row and features; it shows a
+chevron, and its label and value cells nest the slices the way a group nests its children,
+so collapse, reorder within the group, removal and `getParentGroupId` are upstream's group
+code. `rowHandler.addSlices(rowId, parts)` adds slices of the variable's source signal
+(a slice of a slice is a narrower range of the same signal) through `addCustomVariable`;
+`removeSlices` drops them. Both are batched into one context update. Slices are computed in
+the webview from the source signal, like upstream's bit-range signals, with no extra
+document requests. The saved view keeps them as `cmuxSlices: {collapseState, children}` on
+the variable (`SavedSlices` in `packages/vaporview-api/types.ts`), and `addSignalList`
+restores them after the variable. The variable's context adds `cmuxSlices`, `cmuxSliceName`,
+`cmuxMsb` and `cmuxLsb` for the host's menus. cmux's `run.sh split` checks this.
+
+Slices can nest in one request: a part (`SlicePart` in `row_handler.ts`) may carry its own
+`parts`, bits of that slice from 0, so cmux's Split > By Declared Dimensions adds a 3-D
+signal's elements and their elements in one batch, one undo step. The context's
+`cmuxDeclared` is true on a netlist variable (a dump signal the design declares) and false on
+a slice.
